usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
Usage:
  atyrode apply [HOST] [--ref REF | --repo PATH | --candidate STOREPATH] [--git-auth-mode ssh|https-gh] [--plan|--dry-run|--preview-json] [--json] [--restart-shell] [--expected-disruption FINGERPRINT] [--scope SCOPE:SERVICE]...
  atyrode changelog [--json] [--record]
  atyrode apply-status [JOB] [--json]
  atyrode capabilities list [--json]
  atyrode capabilities show [HOST] [--json]
  atyrode runtime list [--json]
  atyrode runtime status [local-qwen|manifold-agent] [--json]
  atyrode runtime provision local-qwen [--data-dir PATH] [--yes]
  atyrode runtime enroll manifold-agent HOST --repo PATH [--rotate-token]
  atyrode runtime provision manifold-agent
  atyrode runtime start|stop|run [local-qwen]
  atyrode runtime start|stop|restart manifold-agent
  atyrode runtime autostart [local-qwen] on|off
  atyrode runtime boot
  atyrode runtime shortcut [local-qwen]
  atyrode fleet plan|apply HOST --repo PATH [--json] [--yes] [--scope SCOPE:SERVICE]...
  atyrode auth broker status [--json]
  atyrode auth broker add-api-key PROVIDER
  atyrode provision machine-key --repo PATH
  atyrode context [show] [--json]
  atyrode context render
  atyrode operator show
  atyrode operator init
  atyrode doctor [HOST] [--json]
  atyrode doctor host [HOST] [--json]
  atyrode doctor system [HOST] [--json]
  atyrode doctor git [--json] [--online]
  atyrode doctor tools [--json]
  atyrode doctor provisioning [--json]
  atyrode host [HOST] [--json]
  atyrode rollback [HOST] [--to N] [--dry-run] [--yes] [--expected-disruption FINGERPRINT] [--scope SCOPE:SERVICE]...
  atyrode clean [--dry-run] [--keep N] [--keep-since DUR] [--yes]
  atyrode windows plan|apply [HOST] [--json]

Fixed hosts keep registry-owned identity; portable bootstrap profiles bind the
validated invoking user and HOME at activation time. apply activates the
published flake by default, resolving REF (default main) to an exact commit
first; --repo switches to a local checkout for development.
apply preview modes are non-activating: --plan resolves and validates the target
then prints preflight metadata without invoking nh; --dry-run invokes the normal
nh switch backend with --dry; --preview-json runs that dry backend and emits its
normalized package/action preview as JSON.
changelog reads what main has that this machine does not: the commits between
the revision this CLI was built from and the head of main, and whether CI has
passed that head. An hourly timer runs it with --record, and every new shell
prints one line from that record until the update is taken; nothing is ever
activated without an operator running apply.
apply builds the candidate closure first, then reads it against the generation
this machine runs -- every systemd unit, including the Home Manager units a
system generation embeds, and every launchd plist -- and reports what the
switch starts, stops, restarts, reloads or retains. That report is the only
authority on whether the switch may proceed: a stop, restart or reload of a
protected service (fleet/service-protection.json, and any unit declaring
X-Atyrode-SessionOwner=true) is refused, as is any impact that could not be
read, in every entry path (apply, background jobs, rollback, fleet and
bootstrap). --preview-json carries the report under `disruption` with a
fingerprint that binds host, both generations and every effect;
--expected-disruption FINGERPRINT refuses to activate anything but that exact
report, and --scope SCOPE:SERVICE (system, user or launchd) refuses any effect
outside the services listed. Neither flag lifts a refusal: there is no
argument that approves disrupting a protected service, and a refused switch
is a deliberate maintenance on the machine itself. --candidate activates a
closure already built and copied here (the fleet's path) after the same
reading. apply validates everything before building, and afterwards reports
plain-omp settings that drifted from the seeded defaults. Mutating Linux Home
Manager applies run as manager-owned jobs rather than as children of the
invoking shell. Started from a terminal the job is handed that terminal:
activation output streams as it happens, every prompt it raises (sudo) is
answerable, and the job ends with the terminal. Without one it is
detached and its output captured, so replacing a terminal-hosting service
cannot terminate the activation and apply-status reconnects to the durable
result. On a terminal apply also offers an interactive keep-or-reset review
after activation, and offers to run the provisioning command for each surface
it finds unconfigured instead of only naming it.
fleet deploys a machine of this clan that the operator is not sitting at:
apply converges the machine it runs on, fleet apply converges another one over
SSH. plan checks that the machine's vars are generated, that it answers a
strict host-key check, and that its configuration evaluates, without activating
anything. apply builds the closure here, copies it there, has the machine read
it against its own running generation and show the disruption report, places
the machine's secrets through clan, and only then activates -- through the
machine's own `atyrode apply --candidate`, bound to the fingerprint just shown
-- and verifies the machine reports itself converged afterwards. Where it is
reached is the machine's own clan.core.networking.targetHost, so a deployment
cannot be aimed somewhere the reviewed configuration does not name. What is
deployed is the checkout named by --repo, always explicit: fleet, provision
machine-key and runtime enroll each commit into or build from a checkout the
operator selected, report its revision and whether its tree is dirty, and
never substitute a conventional ~/nix-dotfiles for it. Plain apply resolves the
selected published revision; doctor inspects the source packaged with its CLI.
doctor git is an offline audit of SSH authentication, signing, forge remotes,
credential helpers, and GitHub CLI storage. --online additionally checks the
selected authentication key's registration with the gh account on github.com.
It reports classifications, paths and fingerprints, never key material,
tokens, helper arguments, or remotes.
context renders this machine's agent context: the operator policy from
modules/home/agents/AGENTS.md with a generated "This machine" section (host, fleet,
which CLIs are authenticated and as whom, readable secrets by name, the fleet
cache, the clone root). render writes ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/agents/AGENTS.md,
which the tool files (~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, ~/.codex/AGENTS.md,
~/.omp/agent/AGENTS.md) link to; show prints it; --json emits the machine
section. It never prints a secret value. apply renders it after activation.
operator is this device's own age identity, the one that edits secrets: every
fixed host holds one, minted where it is used and never copied. On a Mac it is
minted inside the Secure Enclave by age-plugin-se and asks for Touch ID on every
use; elsewhere it is a plain age key protected by the account. show prints its
public recipient and whether clan registers it (sops/users/alex-<host>/key.json,
in group admins); init generates it if absent -- never replacing one that exists
-- and prints the two `clan secrets` commands that register it. alex-recovery,
the software key whose only copy is in Bitwarden, is a member of the group
already and is never read here. A machine's own key, the one its vars are
decrypted with at activation, is clan's: `clan vars generate <host>` mints it on
an operator device and apply places it.
rollback activates an earlier generation of the activation profile (nix-darwin
or NixOS system profile, otherwise standalone Home Manager; previous by default,
or --to N) after reading it against the running generation exactly as apply
does -- --dry-run prints that report -- and confirming.
clean is nh clean for this machine's activation (every profile on NixOS and
nix-darwin, the user's on standalone Home Manager): it drops the generations the
config no longer references, always keeping the current one and a rollback
window (--keep / --keep-since), and collects the store. nh shows its plan and
asks first (--yes skips the prompt, --dry-run only previews); on macOS clean
also sweeps stale Home Manager Apps aliases. Commands reserved for later issues:
workspace and agent.
windows treats WinGet as the native Windows package owner. plan is read-only;
apply reconciles exact package IDs through WSL interop after refusing channel
conflicts. Windows mutations are not part of a Nix generation or rollback.
EOF
}

main() {
  # Before anything reads or drives this machine. Every subcommand asks
  # questions about the programs the machine declares, so none of them should
  # depend on whether the caller's shell has been restarted since activation.
  adopt_activated_path
  # Clan must use the identity operator init owns even in the shell that
  # predates activation; Home Manager session variables require a new login.
  export SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE
  SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE="$(operator_key_file)"
  local subcommand="${1:-help}"
  shift || true
  # A production binary must never mutate the store while a caller believes its
  # tool overrides are in effect (they are not) — refuse before dispatching.
  case "$subcommand" in
    apply | clean | rollback | provision) guard_production_mutation "$subcommand" ;;
  esac
  # A durable record for the commands that change this machine, and only those:
  # a log per `doctor` would bury the runs anyone ever needs to read. The worker
  # keeps its own, because the unit it runs in outlives the shell that asked.
  case "$subcommand" in
    apply | clean | rollback | provision) start_run_log "$subcommand" ;;
    __apply-job) start_run_log apply-job ;;
    operator) [[ "${1:-}" != init ]] || start_run_log "$subcommand" ;;
  esac
  case "$subcommand" in
    apply) apply_config "$@" ;;
    changelog) cmd_changelog "$@" ;;
    __update-notice) changelog_shell_notice ;;
    apply-status) cmd_apply_status "$@" ;;
    __apply-job) run_apply_job_worker "$@" ;;
    __converge-notice) converge_shell_notice ;;
    clean) cmd_clean "$@" ;;
    rollback) cmd_rollback "$@" ;;
    runtime) cmd_runtime "$@" ;;
    fleet) cmd_fleet "$@" ;;
    auth) cmd_auth "$@" ;;
    provision) cmd_provision "$@" ;;
    context) cmd_context "$@" ;;
    operator) [[ "${1:-}" != init ]] || guard_production_mutation "operator init"; cmd_operator "$@" ;;
    capabilities) capabilities "$@" ;;
    doctor)
      case "${1:-}" in
        host) shift; local requested="" json=0; if [[ $# -gt 0 && "$1" != --json ]]; then requested="$1"; shift; fi; [[ "${1:-}" != --json ]] || json=1; doctor_host "$requested" "$json" ;;
        system) shift; doctor_system "$@" ;;
        git) shift; doctor_git "$@" ;;
        tools) shift; doctor_tools "$@" ;;
        provisioning) shift; doctor_provisioning "$@" ;;
        # No subcommand asks the whole question -- "what is missing on this
        # machine" -- which is the one an operator actually has. Anything that
        # is not a family name is a host for the aggregate; resolve_host names
        # the valid ones, so a mistyped family lands on a useful error.
        *) doctor_all "$@" ;;
      esac
      ;;
    host) local requested="" json=0; if [[ $# -gt 0 && "$1" != --json ]]; then requested="$1"; shift; fi; [[ "${1:-}" != --json ]] || json=1; doctor_host "$requested" "$json" ;;
    windows) [[ "${1:-}" != apply ]] || guard_production_mutation "windows apply"; cmd_windows "$@" ;;
    workspace|agent) die "$EX_UNAVAILABLE" "$subcommand is reserved for a follow-up issue" ;;
    help|-h|--help) usage ;;
    *) die "$EX_USAGE" "unknown command: $subcommand" ;;
  esac
}

main "$@"
